WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Phil Wegmann: Good evening ladies and gentleman
my name is Philip Wegmann and I have the pleasure

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
of introducing Ms. Sharyl Attkisson today.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.933
When Ms. Attkisson files stories Washington
DC takes notice.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.133
Her breaking reports on the Fast and Furious
scandal, Benghazi controversy and the blundering

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.333
role of Obama Care continue to capture national
headlines and fuel congressional investigations.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.133
A 30 year veteran of journalism, Ms. Attkisson
has investigated numerous controversies during

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.933
the Clinton, Bush and Obama administrations.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
After graduating from the University of Florida
she first reported with a local PBS network

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.666
then anchored with CNN before beginning her
career as an investigatory journalist with

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
CBS.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.933
Also an accomplished author Attkisson has
recently published a new book called Stonewalled

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
my fight for truth against the forces of obstruction,
intimidation and harassment in Obama’s Washington.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
She has received numerous honors for her work
including the prestigious Edward R. Murrow

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.933
award and also 5 Emmy awards.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.533
Unbiased and unafraid she as tenaciously pursued
truth throughout her entire career.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.866
For this White House officials call our guest
unreasonable however history is more likely

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.666
to call her one of the greatest journalist
of the decade the Woodward and Bernstein to

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
my generation.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.266
Ladies and gentlemen would you please join
me in welcoming Ms. Sharyl Attkisson.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.266
Sharyl Attkisson: I feel so short.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.333
Can you even see me?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
You see my little head that’s good enough
I guess.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.066
Everybody smile!

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.866
I love speaking at colleges.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.066
I just came from the University of Nevada
Reno.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.866
I recently spoke at the Washington Policy
Center, George Mason University.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.533
I’ll be going to colleges in New York, in
Tennessee.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.066
I’ll be at my alma mater University of Florida
Journalism College, Go Gators!, for student

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
conference about freedom of information and
government accountability.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.733
The conference we’re putting on is funded
with some of the proceeds from Stonewalled.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
I’m pleased to have heard from quite a journalism
college professors, not hundreds but a few

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.852
who say that they’ve added Stonewalled to
the recommended reading for the journalism

00:00:02.852 --> 00:00:10.672
students or in the case of a Florida professor
that wrote me an email he said he scrapped

00:00:10.672 --> 00:00:13.652
their textbook and has replaced it with Stonewalled.

00:00:13.652 --> 00:00:19.323
I’m really pleased to hear that some journalism
college professors think there’s something

00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:22.082
of teachable value in there.

00:00:22.082 --> 00:00:24.411
I love learning about the colleges that I
visit.

00:00:24.411 --> 00:00:30.242
One of the interesting facts about Hillsdale
which you all probably know but I found interesting

00:00:30.242 --> 00:00:35.582
is the fact that it turned down the invitation
to a Tangerine Ball in the 1950’s for its

00:00:35.582 --> 00:00:39.411
undefeated football team because Hillsdale’s
black players weren’t going to be allowed

00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:40.562
on the field.

00:00:40.562 --> 00:00:43.551
That’s standing for principle.

00:00:43.551 --> 00:00:46.852
Thank you so much for inviting me.

00:00:46.852 --> 00:00:55.411
In 2009 President Barack Obama pledged to
make history with a high level of transparency

00:00:55.411 --> 00:00:58.082
his administration would bring to government.

00:00:58.082 --> 00:01:03.632
He issued a federal directive stating in part
here’s a quote “My administration is committed

00:01:03.632 --> 00:01:07.832
to creating an unprecedented level of openness
in government.

00:01:07.832 --> 00:01:13.661
We will work together to ensure the public
trust and establish a system of transparency,

00:01:13.661 --> 00:01:16.941
public participation and collaboration.

00:01:16.941 --> 00:01:22.632
Openness will strengthen our democracy and
promote efficiency and effectiveness in government.”

00:01:22.632 --> 00:01:25.992
It sounds so good in theory.

00:01:25.992 --> 00:01:30.551
The Clinton years have proven difficult for
the cause of transparency and any hopes that

00:01:30.551 --> 00:01:35.432
George W. Bush would usher in a new era of
openness were quickly dashed for reporters

00:01:35.432 --> 00:01:41.912
like me but Barack Obama here he was placing
a value and emphasis on openness that really

00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:45.291
to us at least in this business set him apart.

00:01:45.291 --> 00:01:50.191
It could only mean positive things for journalist
especially for investigative journalist like

00:01:50.191 --> 00:01:55.802
me whose effectiveness as watchdogs of government
is directly proportional to our ability to

00:01:55.802 --> 00:02:02.251
access public information and inside sources
at least that’s what we thought.

00:02:02.251 --> 00:02:07.612
The Obama administration has found itself
instead making history for its secrecy and

00:02:07.612 --> 00:02:09.601
assaults on the press.

00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:18.281
There’s delay, denial, obstruction, intimidation,
retaliation, bullying, surveillance and for

00:02:18.281 --> 00:02:21.661
some the possible threat of criminal prosecution.

00:02:21.661 --> 00:02:26.321
In my view and that of other national reporters,
this is proving to be the least transparent

00:02:26.321 --> 00:02:28.612
administration we’ve covered.

00:02:28.612 --> 00:02:34.302
It’s so bad that practically every major
news outlet including the CBS News, Washington

00:02:34.302 --> 00:02:39.892
Post and the New York Times signed a scathing
letter to the White House in 2013 objecting

00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:46.151
to restrictions on the press that are unprecedented
that we had not dealt with before.

00:02:46.151 --> 00:02:52.261
We wrote “As surely as if they were placing
a hand over a journalist camera lens, officials

00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:56.821
in this administration are blocking the public
from having an independent view of important

00:02:56.821 --> 00:03:00.471
functions of the executive branch of government.”

00:03:00.471 --> 00:03:06.441
We called some Obama administration press
policies in this letter “Arbitrary restraint

00:03:06.441 --> 00:03:10.302
and unwarranted interference on legitimate
newsgathering activities.”

00:03:10.302 --> 00:03:15.631
We told the White House “You are in effect
replacing independent photo journalism with

00:03:15.631 --> 00:03:18.131
visual press releases.”

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:22.621
We said the White House behavior raised constitutional
concerns.

00:03:22.621 --> 00:03:26.911
New York Times photographer Doug Mills likened
the White House press office to the Soviet

00:03:26.911 --> 00:03:28.901
news agency Tass.

00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:35.422
It’s so bad that in 2013 the free press
advocacy group Reporters without Borders gave

00:03:35.422 --> 00:03:40.112
a serious downgrade to America’s standing
in the global free press rankings rating the

00:03:40.112 --> 00:03:44.392
Obama administration as worst that Bush’s.

00:03:44.392 --> 00:03:49.142
“The whistleblower is the government’s
enemy.” wrote the group quite rightfully

00:03:49.142 --> 00:03:51.561
explaining it’s finding.

00:03:51.561 --> 00:03:57.092
It said, “Amid an all-out hunt for leaks
and sources, 2013 will be the year of the

00:03:57.092 --> 00:04:01.992
Associated Press scandal which came to light
when the Department of Justice acknowledge

00:04:01.992 --> 00:04:05.431
it had ceased the news agency’s phone records.”

00:04:05.431 --> 00:04:07.691
They weren’t the only ones complaining.

00:04:07.691 --> 00:04:13.011
In March of 2014, New York Times reporter
James Risen spoke at an investigative journalism

00:04:13.011 --> 00:04:18.642
conference I attended at Berkeley and called
the Obama administration “The greatest enemy

00:04:18.642 --> 00:04:23.041
of press freedom in at least a generation.”

00:04:23.041 --> 00:04:26.721
Perhaps the best example I can think of is
to how this administration threatens loss

00:04:26.721 --> 00:04:33.802
of access to control its public message is
a little story that I tell about C-SPAN.

00:04:33.802 --> 00:04:39.052
In summer of 2010 C-SPAN wanted to add footage
of President Obama to a White House documentary

00:04:39.052 --> 00:04:43.911
that had produced in the final years of the
George W. Bush administration.

00:04:43.911 --> 00:04:48.242
President Obama agreed to tape a brief interview
in the oval office with Brian Lamb the founder

00:04:48.242 --> 00:04:52.191
an executive chairman of C-SPAN’s board
of directors.

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:55.631
The 9 minute interview conducted with both
men standing is pretty innocuous.

00:04:55.631 --> 00:04:58.901
“What have you changed in this room?”

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:01.151
Brian Lamb asks the president in the oval
office.

00:05:01.151 --> 00:05:04.261
“We have not redecorated yet,” answers
the president.

00:05:04.261 --> 00:05:08.372
“Given that we are in the midst of some
very difficult economic times we decided to

00:05:08.372 --> 00:05:11.191
hold off last year in terms of making some
changes.”

00:05:11.191 --> 00:05:17.291
All is well until about 2 weeks later when
a C-SPAN official gets a call from Obama’s

00:05:17.291 --> 00:05:21.052
director of broadcast media Dag Vega.

00:05:21.052 --> 00:05:25.832
Vega tells C-SPAN that in just a couple of
days the Washington Post will be breaking

00:05:25.832 --> 00:05:32.011
the story of the president’s reported multimillion
dollar renovation of the oval office.

00:05:32.011 --> 00:05:37.401
Vega is calling to make sure that C-SPAN won't
follow-up that news by running it’s interview

00:05:37.401 --> 00:05:41.981
with the president that seemed to imply the
oposite, the one that was taped just days

00:05:41.981 --> 00:05:42.981
before.

00:05:42.981 --> 00:05:47.382
“You’re going to save the interview for
the documentary which was set to air in a

00:05:47.382 --> 00:05:48.971
few weeks, right?”

00:05:48.971 --> 00:05:52.131
Vega reputedly asked the C-SPAN official.

00:05:52.131 --> 00:05:56.202
“If the public were to see the president’s
interview at that time, they might wonder

00:05:56.202 --> 00:06:00.772
whether he had been clueless or had intentionally
perhaps been misleading about the impending

00:06:00.772 --> 00:06:01.772
make over.”

00:06:01.772 --> 00:06:04.532
From C-SPAN’s viewpoint this is a problem.

00:06:04.532 --> 00:06:08.972
There was never an agreement as to when the
president’s interview would air and it would

00:06:08.972 --> 00:06:14.222
be foolish for C-SPAN to hold the relevant
material only to air the inaccurate interview

00:06:14.222 --> 00:06:17.452
later for the documentary.

00:06:17.452 --> 00:06:21.152
Discussions go back and forth with the White
House saying that the agreement was for the

00:06:21.152 --> 00:06:26.382
president’s interview to air in the future
around the release of the updated documentary

00:06:26.382 --> 00:06:30.772
but within news events C-SPAN rightfully decides
it has no choice but to air the interview

00:06:30.772 --> 00:06:35.642
sooner when the story breaks about the oval
office decorations.

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:40.861
The White House follows up by pressuring C-SPAN
to change its mind and suggests that the cable

00:06:40.861 --> 00:06:47.212
television network will be punished with lack
of access if they do air the interview.

00:06:47.212 --> 00:06:52.671
On August 31st that year as the White House’s
Vega had foretold, the Washington Post breaks

00:06:52.671 --> 00:06:58.082
the news of the president’s oval office
facelift. C-SPAN goes ahead and airs the president’s

00:06:58.082 --> 00:07:00.072
interview the same day.

00:07:00.072 --> 00:07:05.241
That night, Josh Earnest then White House
Deputy Press Secretary now White House Press

00:07:05.241 --> 00:07:10.702
Secretary reportedly fires off an angry email
to C-SPAN.

00:07:10.702 --> 00:07:14.092
The biggest surprise is that he sends it in
the middle of the president’s live address

00:07:14.092 --> 00:07:17.952
to the nation about the drawdown of US troops
from Iraq.

00:07:17.952 --> 00:07:20.222
You’d think he had bigger fish to fry.

00:07:20.222 --> 00:07:26.681
In the email Earnest accuses C-SPAN of being
egregiously unethical in violating terms of

00:07:26.681 --> 00:07:27.681
the interview.

00:07:27.681 --> 00:07:32.702
Though there is no evidence of any existence
of any prior agreement he continues to insist

00:07:32.702 --> 00:07:36.252
the White House would not and did not agree
to an interview with the president without

00:07:36.252 --> 00:07:39.672
specifying all the terms under which it would
air.

00:07:39.672 --> 00:07:43.632
Earnest went on to say that no other news
organization has done such a thing to the

00:07:43.632 --> 00:07:48.462
Obama White House and he threatens to withhold
future access.

00:07:48.462 --> 00:07:53.332
For its part frustrated C-SPAN officials feel
they’re the ones who had been wronged after

00:07:53.332 --> 00:07:58.312
all the president is the one who gave an interview
containing incorrect information in which

00:07:58.312 --> 00:08:01.902
the content was almost immediately invalidated.

00:08:01.902 --> 00:08:06.782
Like a bad tempered child stomping his foot
against the exercise of logic and reason,

00:08:06.782 --> 00:08:11.861
Earnest accuses C-SPAN of a violation of trust
and says there’ll be unlikely to see any

00:08:11.861 --> 00:08:16.861
further cooperation for the president as long
as he remains in office.

00:08:16.861 --> 00:08:20.632
One can only guess whether the Obama White
House has made good on that threat to withhold

00:08:20.632 --> 00:08:26.272
cooperation but C-SPAN’s programing since
that date reflects no interview with either

00:08:26.272 --> 00:08:28.861
the president or the first lady.

00:08:28.861 --> 00:08:33.672
The message, don’t cross the White House
even if it involves the simple act of airing

00:08:33.672 --> 00:08:37.861
an on the record on camera unedited interview
white the president of the United States,

00:08:37.861 --> 00:08:40.491
the consummate public official.

00:08:40.491 --> 00:08:43.972
The White House gets to direct its coverage
and the terms.

00:08:43.972 --> 00:08:46.752
Good behavior will be rewarded with access.

00:08:46.752 --> 00:08:51.572
Dissenters will be punished and I hate to
say it but I think many news organizations

00:08:51.572 --> 00:08:56.152
would have agreed to the White House demand
to hold the president’s interview no questions

00:08:56.152 --> 00:08:58.992
asked.

00:08:58.992 --> 00:09:03.782
President Obama seems to be either oblivious
or in denial or maybe he just thinks that

00:09:03.782 --> 00:09:07.382
repeating the same thing often enough will
make people believe it to be true.

00:09:07.382 --> 00:09:14.752
During an internet question and answer session
hosted by Google on February 14, 2013, the

00:09:14.752 --> 00:09:19.662
president told the online audience this is
the most transparent administration in history.

00:09:19.662 --> 00:09:23.382
Every visitor that comes into the White House
is now part of the public record.

00:09:23.382 --> 00:09:30.332
Every law we pass, every rule we implement
we put online for everyone to see.

00:09:30.332 --> 00:09:35.252
The Obama administration measures its supposed
transparency accomplishments by the sheer

00:09:35.252 --> 00:09:40.022
number of documents published online and the
amount of paper turned over to congress.

00:09:40.022 --> 00:09:45.231
On Benghazi the president says “We’ve
had more testimony and more paper than ever

00:09:45.231 --> 00:09:46.611
before.”

00:09:46.611 --> 00:09:51.702
Never mind all the paper they’re withholding
the ignored and denied freedom of information

00:09:51.702 --> 00:09:56.002
requests or the fact that they still refused
to answer many basic questions more than 2

00:09:56.002 --> 00:10:00.692
years later, the job of getting the truth
has never been harder.

00:10:00.692 --> 00:10:05.991
In part it’s because the Obama administration
and government in general have figured out

00:10:05.991 --> 00:10:11.452
how to avoid questions in accountability by
cutting out the news media middle man.

00:10:11.452 --> 00:10:17.101
White House officials have exploited non news
media to spoon feed unfiltered messaging at

00:10:17.101 --> 00:10:20.632
times pure propaganda into the public’s
mouth.

00:10:20.632 --> 00:10:25.431
The Google sponsored chat that I mentioned
that came with a preselected audience and

00:10:25.431 --> 00:10:29.302
question submitted in advance by the White
House’s own YouTube channel.

00:10:29.302 --> 00:10:31.281
That’s the way they like it.

00:10:31.281 --> 00:10:36.752
They generate their own content, rely on surrogates
to help spread partisan blogs, Twitter and

00:10:36.752 --> 00:10:42.812
Facebook, give lots of interviews to entertainment
programs, digital media and feature press.

00:10:42.812 --> 00:10:47.602
When they feel the situation demands an appearance
of newsiness such as a presidential apology

00:10:47.602 --> 00:10:54.431
for healthcare.gov’s disastrous launch and
obfuscation they look for a soft landing with

00:10:54.431 --> 00:10:57.642
a handpicked outlet reporter.

00:10:57.642 --> 00:11:03.921
All of this impacts how well informed we can
keep the public but also it impacts the very

00:11:03.921 --> 00:11:06.152
survival of investigative journalism.

00:11:06.152 --> 00:11:12.632
In the 3 decades of polling by the Research
Center for people in the press, news organizations

00:11:12.632 --> 00:11:17.272
are near all-time lows when it comes to the
public’s view of our accuracy, fairness

00:11:17.272 --> 00:11:18.911
and independence.

00:11:18.911 --> 00:11:23.712
There’s one thing the public still values
the most and it makes no difference whether

00:11:23.712 --> 00:11:28.702
they’re Democrat, Republican or independent
they overwhelmingly support the media’s

00:11:28.702 --> 00:11:31.822
role as government watchdogs.

00:11:31.822 --> 00:11:39.522
That support rose a full 10 percentage points
from 2011 to 2013 amid revelations about government

00:11:39.522 --> 00:11:43.502
conducted surveillance of the public and the
press.

00:11:43.502 --> 00:11:48.722
The people also reflects the public’s rising
concern about loss of civil liberties.

00:11:48.722 --> 00:11:53.642
If the press doesn’t challenge and expose
government secrecy and overreach then who

00:11:53.642 --> 00:11:55.602
can?

00:11:55.602 --> 00:11:58.152
Back to the broken promise of transparency.

00:11:58.152 --> 00:12:03.012
One of Obama’s first actions in office was
to direct federal agencies to begin complying

00:12:03.012 --> 00:12:05.442
with freedom of information law.

00:12:05.442 --> 00:12:07.281
What a concept!

00:12:07.281 --> 00:12:11.741
No matter where you stand politically as a
journalist one had to be excited by the idea

00:12:11.741 --> 00:12:17.002
that under Obama we would gain back a major
necessary tool to do our jobs.

00:12:17.002 --> 00:12:21.871
Obama said that federal agencies were to err
on the side of releasing public information

00:12:21.871 --> 00:12:24.551
rather than withholding it.

00:12:24.551 --> 00:12:26.272
Instead we’ve seen the opposite.

00:12:26.272 --> 00:12:32.442
A continuation and expansion of the federal
bureaucracy that perverts FOIL law as it’s

00:12:32.442 --> 00:12:37.252
called into a tactic used to prevent the release
of public information.

00:12:37.252 --> 00:12:41.572
To send request to the end of a bureaucratic
queue that may never be answered and if it

00:12:41.572 --> 00:12:47.522
is takes months or years successfully delaying
and obfuscating past the point of relevance.

00:12:47.522 --> 00:12:55.002
I’m still waiting for lawful Reponses to
my FOIL request on Benghazi, Ebola a mysterious

00:12:55.002 --> 00:13:02.692
paralyzing virus, fast and furious, healthcare.gov
from the FBI.

00:13:02.692 --> 00:13:07.351
Try suing over their lack of proper response
and you find the Department of Justice not

00:13:07.351 --> 00:13:13.151
encouraging federal agencies to abide by the
spirit of FOIL law but instead providing lawyers

00:13:13.151 --> 00:13:19.392
to defend the withholding of information and
they use your tax dollars for the court cases

00:13:19.392 --> 00:13:21.741
so there’s no downside for them.

00:13:21.741 --> 00:13:26.541
They still accomplish the obfuscation and
delay and in the end even if the court orders

00:13:26.541 --> 00:13:30.562
them to turn over document there is no punishment
or recourse.

00:13:30.562 --> 00:13:34.712
They may have to pay the plaintiff’s legal
fees but they do that with your tax dollars

00:13:34.712 --> 00:13:36.092
too.

00:13:36.092 --> 00:13:42.442
It’s not just me and it’s not an opinion,
it’s a fact and a consensus.

00:13:42.442 --> 00:13:46.662
In late June 2013 I’m flying back from an
investigator reporter’s conference in San

00:13:46.662 --> 00:13:50.671
Antonio, Texas and I’m seated next to another
journalist Len Downie.

00:13:50.671 --> 00:13:55.332
He’s the former executive of the Washington
Post who had just spoken at our conference.

00:13:55.332 --> 00:14:01.231
He had told the audience “The Obama administration’s
war on leaks is by far the most aggressive

00:14:01.231 --> 00:14:06.731
that I’ve seen since the Nixon administration
and I go back that far.”

00:14:06.731 --> 00:14:10.942
As Downie and I chat shoulder to shoulder
on the airplane I bring up the subject of

00:14:10.942 --> 00:14:12.452
Edward Snowden.

00:14:12.452 --> 00:14:17.121
I asked Downie if it doesn’t seem as though
more tension should be focused on the content

00:14:17.121 --> 00:14:22.443
of Snowden’s claims instead of only whether
he’s hiding, whether he broke the law, whether

00:14:22.443 --> 00:14:25.062
he graduated from high school.

00:14:25.062 --> 00:14:26.212
Downie agrees.

00:14:26.212 --> 00:14:30.662
Four months later Downie published what I
see is the definitive report for the committee

00:14:30.662 --> 00:14:32.832
to protect journalist.

00:14:32.832 --> 00:14:37.732
It establishes the Obama administration as
the news media’s top choice for least transparent

00:14:37.732 --> 00:14:41.232
American presidency in modern times.

00:14:41.232 --> 00:14:44.863
When you think of all the transparency promises,
it’s pretty stunning the way the actual

00:14:44.863 --> 00:14:50.082
experiences of national news reporters not
those working at conservative outlets but

00:14:50.082 --> 00:14:55.122
journalist from the New York Times, the Washington
Post.

00:14:55.122 --> 00:14:59.882
David Sanger our chief Washington correspondent
at the time said “This is the most closed

00:14:59.882 --> 00:15:03.682
control freak administration I’ve ever covered.”

00:15:03.682 --> 00:15:09.212
Times public editor Margaret Sullivan “It’s
turning out to be the administration of unprecedented

00:15:09.212 --> 00:15:13.692
secrecy and unprecedented attacks on a free
press.”

00:15:13.692 --> 00:15:18.392
Financial Times correspondent Richard McGregor
“Covering this White House is pretty miserable

00:15:18.392 --> 00:15:22.582
in terms of getting anything of substance
to report on in what should be a much more

00:15:22.582 --> 00:15:24.182
open system.”

00:15:24.182 --> 00:15:29.852
ABC News White House correspondent Ann Compton
“He’s the least transparent of the 7 presidents

00:15:29.852 --> 00:15:33.532
that I’ve covered in terms of how he does
his daily business.”

00:15:33.532 --> 00:15:37.902
Josh Gerstein of Politico “If the story
is basically one that they don’t want to

00:15:37.902 --> 00:15:41.672
come out, they won't even give you the basic
facts.”

00:15:41.672 --> 00:15:46.672
Washington correspondent Josh Meyer “There
is across the board hostility to the media.

00:15:46.672 --> 00:15:52.312
They don’t return repeated phone calls and
emails they feel entitled to and expect supportive

00:15:52.312 --> 00:15:54.962
media coverage.”

00:15:54.962 --> 00:16:00.842
Post managing editor Kevin Merida describes
what he sees “As the White House’s hypersensitivity

00:16:00.842 --> 00:16:05.612
saying the officials often call reporters
and editors to complain about the tiniest

00:16:05.612 --> 00:16:10.402
thing maybe something on Twitter or a headline
on a website.

00:16:10.402 --> 00:16:14.392
I have a slightly different interpretation
of the administration sensitivities and all

00:16:14.392 --> 00:16:15.392
their reactions.

00:16:15.392 --> 00:16:19.832
It’s not really that they’re so sensitive,
they’re simply executing a well thought

00:16:19.832 --> 00:16:25.212
out strategy to harass reporters and editors
at the slightly error of negativity so as

00:16:25.212 --> 00:16:28.002
to impact the next news decisions.

00:16:28.002 --> 00:16:33.502
To provide so much unpleasant static and interference,
that we may subconsciously alter the way we

00:16:33.502 --> 00:16:35.752
report stories.

00:16:35.752 --> 00:16:41.102
To consume so much of our time explaining
and justifying what we reported that we begin

00:16:41.102 --> 00:16:43.272
to self-censor in the future.

00:16:43.272 --> 00:16:48.932
They accuse us of “piling on” when all
we’re doing is accurately covering their

00:16:48.932 --> 00:16:53.792
actions and the outcome of their decisions
but what human being doesn’t instinctively

00:16:53.792 --> 00:16:58.062
learn to avoid negative unpleasant feedback.”

00:16:58.062 --> 00:17:02.942
This trend did not begin with the Obama administration
and I’m afraid will not end there either.

00:17:02.942 --> 00:17:08.052
I don’t really blame the Obama administration
and I don’t even blame the entrenched federal

00:17:08.052 --> 00:17:09.052
bureaucracy.

00:17:09.052 --> 00:17:12.802
I blame us the news media.

00:17:12.802 --> 00:17:17.332
When government invariably attempts to advance
itself, separate itself from the public it

00:17:17.332 --> 00:17:22.362
works for, covets information, feathers its
own nest and acts as if it’s an independent

00:17:22.362 --> 00:17:28.682
corporation beholden to shareholders, politicians
and special interest rather than public, it’s

00:17:28.682 --> 00:17:32.102
the media that’s supposed to make sure that
doesn’t get out of balance.

00:17:32.102 --> 00:17:34.812
We haven’t been doing a very good job.

00:17:34.812 --> 00:17:36.162
We send letters.

00:17:36.162 --> 00:17:41.202
We make statements but instead of insisting
our rights be respected we place ourselves

00:17:41.202 --> 00:17:48.062
in an unnecessarily subservient position as
if it’s a favor we’re asking for, something

00:17:48.062 --> 00:17:53.782
to be granted at the king’s pleasure rather
than a right that we’re entitled to.

00:17:53.782 --> 00:17:58.282
Instead of demanding the government follow
the law, we politely and somewhat timidly

00:17:58.282 --> 00:18:02.592
ask “Could you pretty please follow the
law?”

00:18:02.592 --> 00:18:07.332
Instead of demanding to access to information
we own, that’s collected on our behalf by

00:18:07.332 --> 00:18:13.052
public servants paid with tax dollars we make
an appeal “Could you maybe please just consider

00:18:13.052 --> 00:18:15.122
giving us some of our information?”

00:18:15.122 --> 00:18:21.202
Here I’d like to pause and take some questions
on this topic before speaking a little bit

00:18:21.202 --> 00:18:22.382
on a related topic.

00:18:22.382 --> 00:18:27.642
How the government generates its own propaganda
using your tax dollars and why what you see

00:18:27.642 --> 00:18:32.952
on the news is so homogenous and often discourages
a very original reporting that would actually

00:18:32.952 --> 00:18:34.282
get more viewers.

00:18:34.282 --> 00:18:37.472
We’ll take questions for a few minutes and
then I’ll talk about that.

00:18:37.472 --> 00:18:42.632
There’s some nice young ladies in the aisles
ready to hear from you.

00:18:42.632 --> 00:18:44.592
Yes sir.

00:18:44.592 --> 00:18:48.992
Speaker 1: Thank you very much.

00:18:48.992 --> 00:18:49.992
Quick question.

00:18:49.992 --> 00:18:54.832
It seems to be part of the problem seems to
be that we do not have a loyal opposition

00:18:54.832 --> 00:19:00.502
anywhere in this government relative to what
the people want and demand about liberty.

00:19:00.502 --> 00:19:08.102
Sharyl Attkisson: I would say there is a shared
viewpoint among a lot of people that even

00:19:08.102 --> 00:19:16.332
the Republican “opposition” is not doing
its best job at presenting opposing viewpoints.

00:19:16.332 --> 00:19:21.162
I will say that I’ve noticed this is just
my opinion based on observations but also

00:19:21.162 --> 00:19:25.102
conversations of what staffers and members
of congress.

00:19:25.102 --> 00:19:32.052
Like the media there seems to be less will
to do oversight, to ask the tough questions

00:19:32.052 --> 00:19:35.232
and do the follow-through.

00:19:35.232 --> 00:19:40.132
Much like the media I hear staffers and members
of congress saying the same things.

00:19:40.132 --> 00:19:46.152
There just isn’t the appetite and leadership
to do tough oversight.

00:19:46.152 --> 00:19:48.132
They want to take the easy road.

00:19:48.132 --> 00:19:52.282
They do look into things but only goes so
far and then they seem not to follow up.

00:19:52.282 --> 00:19:59.312
I’ve made some of those same observations
and I feel like there’s some symmetry with

00:19:59.312 --> 00:20:09.092
what’s going on in some aspects with the
media as well.

00:20:09.092 --> 00:20:11.942
Let the lady with the microphone decide who
to go to.

00:20:11.942 --> 00:20:18.962
Speaker 2: Can you shed some light and give
some insight on why the Obama administration

00:20:18.962 --> 00:20:19.962
is so untransparent?

00:20:19.962 --> 00:20:25.322
Do they have things to hide or they just constitutionally
not inclined to shed any information on what

00:20:25.322 --> 00:20:26.322
they’re doing?

00:20:26.322 --> 00:20:28.792
Give us some insight into that please.

00:20:28.792 --> 00:20:32.472
Sharyl Attkisson: It’s hard to know what
people are hiding when they don’t tell you

00:20:32.472 --> 00:20:35.082
what they’re hiding.

00:20:35.082 --> 00:20:39.432
I would argue it’s a natural inclination
of those in power to do that.

00:20:39.432 --> 00:20:44.712
I believe when President Obama said he was
going to usher in a new era of transparency

00:20:44.712 --> 00:20:46.432
I believe he meant it.

00:20:46.432 --> 00:20:51.682
I believe he thought he could and something
happens along the way whether it’s embarrassing

00:20:51.682 --> 00:20:58.142
information they’d rather not have be released
or they get inundated by advisers who have

00:20:58.142 --> 00:21:03.112
different opinions but it seems to be that
no matter how good the intentions are the

00:21:03.112 --> 00:21:04.702
opposite happens.

00:21:04.702 --> 00:21:09.632
That’s where again as I said I felt the
press, if that’s the natural inclination

00:21:09.632 --> 00:21:14.322
of government it’s our job as the media
to push back against that and make sure it’s

00:21:14.322 --> 00:21:18.282
not successful if they’re withholding information
that they shouldn’t be withholding.

00:21:18.282 --> 00:21:21.962
We’re supposed to dig and probe and push
on that and I don’t think we’ve always

00:21:21.962 --> 00:21:24.532
done the best job at that.

00:21:24.532 --> 00:21:41.122
Speaker 3: I just want to follow up on this
gentleman’s question because it was kind

00:21:41.122 --> 00:21:43.322
of what I was thinking originally.

00:21:43.322 --> 00:21:53.552
What I’m concerned about is this president
has made so many decisions that really are

00:21:53.552 --> 00:22:00.462
not in the best interest of the United States
and that’s what concerns me.

00:22:00.462 --> 00:22:03.692
That’s what he’s hiding.

00:22:03.692 --> 00:22:14.172
Benghazi, the national security all of his
decision leave me to think there’s something

00:22:14.172 --> 00:22:16.812
very nafty going on.

00:22:16.812 --> 00:22:22.082
Sharyl Attkisson: As a journalist when someone
doesn’t want to tell you something especially

00:22:22.082 --> 00:22:27.792
when it’s arguably public information that’s
what sets of the idea that they’re wanting

00:22:27.792 --> 00:22:31.592
to hide something for a reason even if you
don’t know what it is but that’s certainly

00:22:31.592 --> 00:22:35.712
what keeps you going in my case what keeps
me digging.

00:22:35.712 --> 00:22:40.852
On the other hand I would say there’s still
about 50% of America that would disagree with

00:22:40.852 --> 00:22:41.852
you.

00:22:41.852 --> 00:22:48.132
I have a very active social media account
and for all the people who sound exactly like

00:22:48.132 --> 00:22:58.122
you, I have postings from reasonable acquaintances
who say quite the opposite, who think that

00:22:58.122 --> 00:23:02.842
and you’ve heard it too, they still think
this president is correct in what he’s done

00:23:02.842 --> 00:23:08.492
that the mistakes and problems are overblown
by a partisan press and by Republican stoked

00:23:08.492 --> 00:23:10.082
controversies and so on.

00:23:10.082 --> 00:23:17.142
I guess there’s just difference of opinion
on that.

00:23:17.142 --> 00:23:22.714
Speaker 4: Thank you very much.

00:23:22.714 --> 00:23:28.422
You mentioned that there’s less will to
do oversight no appetite, they want to take

00:23:28.422 --> 00:23:29.742
the easy road.

00:23:29.742 --> 00:23:36.202
I assume you’re talking about the individual
journalist in this case and that certainly

00:23:36.202 --> 00:23:41.262
the symptoms but I’m wondering a little
deeper if you can give us your thought on

00:23:41.262 --> 00:23:47.782
the corporation influence on each of those
journalist within CBS, ABC, NBC you amen them

00:23:47.782 --> 00:23:49.912
all the 3 letters.

00:23:49.912 --> 00:23:51.722
You see some of them coming out.

00:23:51.722 --> 00:23:53.022
You see yourself coming out.

00:23:53.022 --> 00:23:59.652
I see Fox News coming out that everybody else
takes the high road if you will and stays

00:23:59.652 --> 00:24:01.472
away from the real problem.

00:24:01.472 --> 00:24:04.812
Why does the corporations allow that?

00:24:04.812 --> 00:24:11.932
Sharyl Attkisson: When I talk about the taking
the path of least resistance, I don’t particularly

00:24:11.932 --> 00:24:14.632
mean the individual ground level journalist.

00:24:14.632 --> 00:24:19.682
I think I’m speaking more and I’ll talk
about this in a few minutes about the gatekeepers

00:24:19.682 --> 00:24:25.902
who decide what goes on TV and how much headache
they want to have against these highly sophisticated

00:24:25.902 --> 00:24:31.832
fairly recent pushback campaigns that are
well funded, that are mounted by special interest

00:24:31.832 --> 00:24:35.992
and corporate interest against new stories
they don’t like and news outlets that bother

00:24:35.992 --> 00:24:40.952
to publish these things and whistleblowers
who dare to tell the truth and politicians

00:24:40.952 --> 00:24:42.842
who dare to ask the tough questions.

00:24:42.842 --> 00:24:48.802
They’re subjected to an organized campaign
to controversialize them funded by PR efforts

00:24:48.802 --> 00:24:54.052
that are invisible so it appears to be a grassroots
over swelling opinion that’s not really

00:24:54.052 --> 00:24:55.052
there.

00:24:55.052 --> 00:24:59.432
I call it an artificial reality but it’s
somewhat overwhelming to the news organizations

00:24:59.432 --> 00:25:04.752
at times who see all the social media activity
and read the logs and read about themselves

00:25:04.752 --> 00:25:07.992
and maybe don’t have the stomach to push
through that.

00:25:07.992 --> 00:25:10.632
It’s hard to say how much corporate influence
there is.

00:25:10.632 --> 00:25:15.712
There’s always been a little bit of that
and journalist from time to time have to pushback

00:25:15.712 --> 00:25:22.842
against what we feel maybe corporate influence
but you don’t always know what corporate

00:25:22.842 --> 00:25:28.192
influence is because no one tells you necessarily
why certain decisions are made.

00:25:28.192 --> 00:25:32.462
In a few instances in the book I talk about
… I did have some insight because somebody

00:25:32.462 --> 00:25:33.882
said something very pointed.

00:25:33.882 --> 00:25:38.923
In general you don’t always know why the
gatekeepers don’t want a particular story

00:25:38.923 --> 00:25:45.362
that seems so obvious and so obviously in
the public interest.

00:25:45.362 --> 00:25:52.072
I would say corporations and government have
become so intertwined in their interest and

00:25:52.072 --> 00:25:57.012
they become intertwined also with the media
which is beholden to in some respects the

00:25:57.012 --> 00:26:00.432
interest of the government and some corporations
as well.

00:26:00.432 --> 00:26:06.652
Whether they’re advertisers or some other
interest it’s all meddling into one big

00:26:06.652 --> 00:26:09.082
interest.

00:26:09.082 --> 00:26:12.972
When they all start to think alike and don’t
want to do oversight on each other because

00:26:12.972 --> 00:26:17.912
they’re beholden to the same ideas or interest
I think that’s part of a trend you might

00:26:17.912 --> 00:26:19.832
be seeing.

00:26:19.832 --> 00:26:24.492
Speaker 5: Thank you so much Sharyl.

00:26:24.492 --> 00:26:31.342
I was wondering if you could perhaps compare
the relationship between Obama’s administration

00:26:31.342 --> 00:26:41.842
and the press with perhaps past presidents
like say President Woodrow Wilson or Abraham

00:26:41.842 --> 00:26:42.842
Lincoln even.

00:26:42.842 --> 00:26:47.092
Sharyl Attkisson: I don’t have an answer
for that question I’m sorry because I’m

00:26:47.092 --> 00:26:50.062
not a student of those times.

00:26:50.062 --> 00:26:53.042
I can’t say.

00:26:53.042 --> 00:26:58.262
Every chunk of press has its own relationship
with the president and there’ve always been

00:26:58.262 --> 00:27:00.302
adversarial questions asked.

00:27:00.302 --> 00:27:04.812
I think back to Sam Donaldson “Hold on Mr.
President!”

00:27:04.812 --> 00:27:08.952
and there are still reporters asking adversarial
questions even if they don’t make it on

00:27:08.952 --> 00:27:11.882
the evening news necessarily every night.

00:27:11.882 --> 00:27:17.142
I’m sorry I haven’t studied back that
far.

00:27:17.142 --> 00:27:28.242
Speaker 6: Within the context of what you
just said about a lot of reasons for skepticism

00:27:28.242 --> 00:27:34.032
about corporate America and about the government,
previously you commented that you gave Obama

00:27:34.032 --> 00:27:41.982
the benefit of the doubt when you said that
he would be transparent, what’s the basis

00:27:41.982 --> 00:27:44.032
of that opinion?

00:27:44.032 --> 00:27:52.702
Sharyl Attkisson: Once he was already elected
to make that be a key proclamation as you

00:27:52.702 --> 00:27:57.472
enter office without any reason for having
to say so once you already elected but to

00:27:57.472 --> 00:28:01.762
focus so much attention on that I do give
the benefit of the doubt.

00:28:01.762 --> 00:28:06.812
I could be wrong but my instinct say he meant
it and he thought that was one thing that

00:28:06.812 --> 00:28:09.162
he could change about Washington that was
a problem.

00:28:09.162 --> 00:28:14.462
It didn’t turn out that way and I can’t
tell you what’s really in his mind or his

00:28:14.462 --> 00:28:17.292
heart that’s just my interpretation.

00:28:17.292 --> 00:28:35.582
Dave: Hi my name is Dave Fergusson and I’m
just wondering if you know for sure whether

00:28:35.582 --> 00:28:43.252
or not this president has college records
sealed and if so would that be a clear indication

00:28:43.252 --> 00:28:46.692
of how transparent this guy was going to be?

00:28:46.692 --> 00:28:48.922
Sharyl Attkisson: I have not looked into that
issue.

00:28:48.922 --> 00:28:49.922
Sorry.

00:28:49.922 --> 00:28:53.592
I’m going to try not to answer questions
I don’t know much about.

00:28:53.592 --> 00:28:59.792
There if you don’t mind let me go on to
the second half of what I thought you might

00:28:59.792 --> 00:29:06.652
find interesting to talk about and then I’ll
take some more questions after that.

00:29:06.652 --> 00:29:10.782
This gets to the heart of a few questions
you all just asked.

00:29:10.782 --> 00:29:15.322
In the last decade or more the government
has adopted and perfected many of the public

00:29:15.322 --> 00:29:19.852
relations and crisis management strategies
employed by big corporation.

00:29:19.852 --> 00:29:23.812
It’s a natural outgrowth of their incestuous
relationship.

00:29:23.812 --> 00:29:28.852
The big difference is the government is using
your tax dollars to promote itself and advance

00:29:28.852 --> 00:29:31.242
it’s propaganda.

00:29:31.242 --> 00:29:36.462
One way they do it by self-producing videos
and building their very own television production

00:29:36.462 --> 00:29:42.292
facilities where the upper echelon gives interviews
and speeches controlling everything from content

00:29:42.292 --> 00:29:44.252
to lighting.

00:29:44.252 --> 00:29:49.282
While the nation descended into unprecedented
debt, congress and the federal agencies ranging

00:29:49.282 --> 00:29:54.292
from health and human services to the national
institutes of health used millions of your

00:29:54.292 --> 00:29:59.642
tax dollars to build or expand their very
own television studios.

00:29:59.642 --> 00:30:05.222
The food and drug administration’s facility
boast “a number of mobile and fixed sets

00:30:05.222 --> 00:30:10.452
as well as various configurations to allow
for a studio audience of over 100.”

00:30:10.452 --> 00:30:16.912
The Transportation Security Administration,
TSA, sports a studio with Hitachi high definition

00:30:16.912 --> 00:30:22.782
cameras and Fujinon lenses and LCD based teleprompters.

00:30:22.782 --> 00:30:26.872
When top officials from those federal agencies
appear on camera, naturally they have to look

00:30:26.872 --> 00:30:27.872
good.

00:30:27.872 --> 00:30:33.032
Your tax dollars may kick in for the cost
of their hairstylist, makeup artist and wardrobe

00:30:33.032 --> 00:30:35.082
consultants.

00:30:35.082 --> 00:30:40.022
One insider told me that the head of a federal
agency even had her fashion colors analyzed

00:30:40.022 --> 00:30:42.822
at tax payer expense.

00:30:42.822 --> 00:30:48.052
In addition the Pentagon has its own 24 hour
channel which features military news, interviews

00:30:48.052 --> 00:30:51.992
with top defense officials and program such
as the Grill Sergeants.

00:30:51.992 --> 00:30:57.172
While the Pentagon frets over sequestration
cuts and the troops listen to talk of cutting

00:30:57.172 --> 00:31:02.172
their pensions, your tax dollars are paying
to produce programs such as a cooking show

00:31:02.172 --> 00:31:07.362
competition that features mess hall cooks
and aids to generals battling it out over

00:31:07.362 --> 00:31:13.332
dishes like seared ahi tuna and lamb with
blueberry wine sauce.

00:31:13.332 --> 00:31:18.042
Both the defense department and the centers
for disease control provide tax payer funded

00:31:18.042 --> 00:31:24.452
advisers to television and Hollywood entertainment
producers to promote accuracy or propaganda

00:31:24.452 --> 00:31:27.242
depending on your viewpoint.

00:31:27.242 --> 00:31:31.012
Some of the public interest justification
for use of these assets are dubious.

00:31:31.012 --> 00:31:37.802
In 2013 you may remember congress caught the
IRS making Star Trek and Gilligan’s island

00:31:37.802 --> 00:31:42.802
parody videos to educate federal employees
at a conference.

00:31:42.802 --> 00:31:47.672
When Secretary of Energy Steven Chu resigned
from the Obama administration, the federal

00:31:47.672 --> 00:31:51.892
agency produced a slick photo tribute to him
using your money.

00:31:51.892 --> 00:31:57.712
It touts Chu’s incredible successes but
forgets to mention any of his scandals such

00:31:57.712 --> 00:32:03.102
as the failed effort at playing venture capitalist
with tax dollars and green energy investments

00:32:03.102 --> 00:32:04.552
like Solyndra.

00:32:04.552 --> 00:32:10.832
By its own admission the Pentagon the film
liaison office gives for profit filmmakers

00:32:10.832 --> 00:32:16.872
free use of taxpayer assets from tanks to
jets but only if the film portrays the images

00:32:16.872 --> 00:32:18.392
the Pentagon wants.

00:32:18.392 --> 00:32:20.242
If not, the assets are withheld.

00:32:20.242 --> 00:32:26.862
There was a documentary called Hollywood and
Pentagon a dangerous liaison.

00:32:26.862 --> 00:32:31.652
In the documentary they said “Scripts are
cut and sometimes watered down.

00:32:31.652 --> 00:32:35.982
Characters are changed and historical truths
sometimes fudged.

00:32:35.982 --> 00:32:39.532
One director might be loaned combat ships
and jets.

00:32:39.532 --> 00:32:45.102
Another director who’s script displeases
the government or the army maybe refused any

00:32:45.102 --> 00:32:46.912
kind of support.

00:32:46.912 --> 00:32:51.472
Few great war films have escaped the influence
or even the censure of the US army.”

00:32:51.472 --> 00:32:57.302
It’s pure propaganda according to the documentary
and you’re paying for it.

00:32:57.302 --> 00:33:02.662
Like big corporations each federal agency
and all 535 members of congress have teams

00:33:02.662 --> 00:33:07.992
of taxpayer funded media and communication
specialist to advance their message.

00:33:07.992 --> 00:33:13.212
A few years ago a well-placed insider at the
US Department of Agriculture confessed to

00:33:13.212 --> 00:33:18.872
me that even he was surprised to learn that
his own agency supposedly had more than 1200

00:33:18.872 --> 00:33:24.752
employees working in some sort of media relations
capacity nationwide.

00:33:24.752 --> 00:33:29.562
Department of Agriculture, 1200 media relations
employees.

00:33:29.562 --> 00:33:34.122
Your tax dollars pay for their salaries that
many times they’re little more than private

00:33:34.122 --> 00:33:40.032
publicity agents for their bosses spending,
avoiding and obfuscating as expertly as any

00:33:40.032 --> 00:33:45.402
of their corporate counterparts and too often
we let them get away with it.

00:33:45.402 --> 00:33:50.442
Too often press releases that read like propaganda
from government and corporation are used in

00:33:50.442 --> 00:33:55.952
the news without the required critical examination
and analysis.

00:33:55.952 --> 00:33:59.202
If you’re confused about all the influences
behind what you see the news and how they

00:33:59.202 --> 00:34:02.832
affect the product, there’s good reason.

00:34:02.832 --> 00:34:07.432
At times there’s a liberal political bias
in mainstream media that tells toward stories

00:34:07.432 --> 00:34:12.812
favoring liberal social issues and philosophies
but there’s also a competing conservative

00:34:12.812 --> 00:34:19.412
corporate bias that favors specific companies,
industries and paid interest.

00:34:19.412 --> 00:34:22.592
Unfortunately the result isn’t an ideal
balance of complete information about the

00:34:22.592 --> 00:34:27.022
world, it’s often a distorted and perplexing
mix.

00:34:27.022 --> 00:34:31.082
The trend has become more predominant in the
last couple of years as powerful interests

00:34:31.082 --> 00:34:35.842
have mastered their method of influencing
us and some of our managers have embraced

00:34:35.842 --> 00:34:40.872
the influence believing they’ll keep their
lucrative jobs by going along rather than

00:34:40.872 --> 00:34:42.982
resisting.

00:34:42.982 --> 00:34:47.462
The capitulation to special interest may preserve
these news manager’s job into short term

00:34:47.462 --> 00:34:52.172
but in the big picture I think they’re ensuring
a quicker demise of the entire platform.

00:34:52.172 --> 00:34:57.362
Alienating and eroding the audience that we
supposedly serve.

00:34:57.362 --> 00:35:01.782
The network evening newscast bragged that
increasing numbers of people are watching,

00:35:01.782 --> 00:35:06.712
the total number of television broadcast network
audience members compared to that which is

00:35:06.712 --> 00:35:09.882
available remains minuscule.

00:35:09.882 --> 00:35:12.462
Many in the public believe are feeding them
a lot of pablum.

00:35:12.462 --> 00:35:16.772
I’ve never before heard so many people say
so, liberals, conservatives and people who

00:35:16.772 --> 00:35:19.712
define themselves as neither.

00:35:19.712 --> 00:35:24.432
What is the mysterious process behind the
decisions as to what stories make the news

00:35:24.432 --> 00:35:26.942
on the given night?

00:35:26.942 --> 00:35:31.882
Some stories are carefully chosen and edited
by a small group of broadcast news managers

00:35:31.882 --> 00:35:33.982
because they serve a specific set of agendas.

00:35:33.982 --> 00:35:41.852
In a book called Manufacturing Consent the
author state commercial news organizations

00:35:41.852 --> 00:35:48.862
disseminate propaganda on behalf of dominant
private interest in the government “The

00:35:48.862 --> 00:35:54.192
media do not function in the manner of the
propaganda system of a totalitarian state,

00:35:54.192 --> 00:36:00.382
rather they permit indeed encourage spirited
debate, criticism and dissent as long as these

00:36:00.382 --> 00:36:07.522
remain faithfully within the system of presuppositions
and principles that constitute an elite consensus.

00:36:07.522 --> 00:36:11.102
A system so powerful as to be internalized
largely without awareness.”

00:36:11.102 --> 00:36:14.282
There are other factors at play.

00:36:14.282 --> 00:36:19.352
Many story topics are selected by managers
who are as I say producing out of fear in

00:36:19.352 --> 00:36:21.942
trying to play it safe.

00:36:21.942 --> 00:36:26.122
Playing it safe means airing stories that
certain other trusted media have reported

00:36:26.122 --> 00:36:29.782
first so there’s no perceived risk to us
if we report them too.

00:36:29.782 --> 00:36:31.722
We’re not going out on a limb.

00:36:31.722 --> 00:36:35.552
We’re not reporting anything that hasn’t
already been reported elsewhere but it also

00:36:35.552 --> 00:36:39.262
means we’re not giving viewers a reason
to watch us.

00:36:39.262 --> 00:36:45.382
Playing it safe means shying away from stories
that include allegations against certain corporations,

00:36:45.382 --> 00:36:49.912
charities and other chosen powerful entities
in people.

00:36:49.912 --> 00:36:55.122
The image of the news media as fearless watchdogs
poised if not eager to pursue stories that

00:36:55.122 --> 00:36:59.092
authorities wish to block is often a false
image.

00:36:59.092 --> 00:37:04.512
Decisions are routinely made in fear of the
response that the story might provoke.

00:37:04.512 --> 00:37:07.302
The propaganda’s heavy handed tactics have
worked.

00:37:07.302 --> 00:37:10.692
They don’t even have to pick up the phone
and complain about a story.

00:37:10.692 --> 00:37:16.532
Some news managers demonstrate a Pavlovian
style avoidance response when presented with

00:37:16.532 --> 00:37:20.342
a story that they fear will bring some negative
reaction.

00:37:20.342 --> 00:37:24.762
We are weak and diffident when we needn’t
be.

00:37:24.762 --> 00:37:28.082
Many investigative reporters around the nation
are experiencing the same thing.

00:37:28.082 --> 00:37:29.842
It’s a trend.

00:37:29.842 --> 00:37:35.461
Long time Emmy Award winning reporter Al Sunshine
retired from the CBS owned and operated station

00:37:35.461 --> 00:37:38.892
in Miami the summer before I left CBS News.

00:37:38.892 --> 00:37:43.872
Afterward he made some similar observations
he said “Because of the recent lack of support

00:37:43.872 --> 00:37:49.032
and commitment for my investigations, I faced
an almost daily battle to get the time to

00:37:49.032 --> 00:37:53.402
work on my stories and had to fight harder
than ever for air time.”

00:37:53.402 --> 00:37:58.462
Though his brand of investigative reporting
in consumer stories was widely popular with

00:37:58.462 --> 00:38:03.822
viewers, sometimes resulting in new laws being
passed and criminals getting prosecuted, he

00:38:03.822 --> 00:38:07.362
was told his stories were too negative.

00:38:07.362 --> 00:38:12.452
Instead he was often reassigned to what we
call day of air news coverage.

00:38:12.452 --> 00:38:17.902
He told me “Advertisers are dominating news
judgment and news organizations all over the

00:38:17.902 --> 00:38:18.902
country.

00:38:18.902 --> 00:38:22.832
The public interest is being diminished in
the interest of corporate advertisers and

00:38:22.832 --> 00:38:23.832
lobbyist.

00:38:23.832 --> 00:38:28.882
What’s almost universally accepted is business
as usual in Washington, corruption between

00:38:28.882 --> 00:38:33.711
lobbyist dollars and political favoritism
is slowly but surely becoming the norm for

00:38:33.711 --> 00:38:38.972
many news organizations as well.” says Sunshine.

00:38:38.972 --> 00:38:43.282
It might be growing worse but historical narrative
implies there’s always been an element of

00:38:43.282 --> 00:38:47.502
this condition avoidance response in the corporate
news world.

00:38:47.502 --> 00:38:54.452
In his 1967 memoir called Due to circumstances
beyond our control CBS news president Fred

00:38:54.452 --> 00:39:00.072
Friendly expressed discomfort that top management
felt over its star reporter at the time Edward

00:39:00.072 --> 00:39:01.762
R. Murrow.

00:39:01.762 --> 00:39:10.211
During the 1954-55 season we did a 2 part
report on cigarettes and lung cancer and both

00:39:10.211 --> 00:39:16.302
CBS and CBS sponsor Alcoa, aluminum company
felt the pressures of the tobacco industry

00:39:16.302 --> 00:39:19.432
which buys both air time and aluminum foil.

00:39:19.432 --> 00:39:26.532
The attitude at CBS says this book was “Why
does Murrow have to save the world every week?”

00:39:26.532 --> 00:39:32.182
In another instance, Fred Friendly quoted
CBS Bill Paley as telling Edward R. Murrow

00:39:32.182 --> 00:39:38.802
“I don’t want this constant stomachache
every time you do a controversial subject.”

00:39:38.802 --> 00:39:44.022
These tendencies to censor topics that generates
objections from their powerful targets aren’t

00:39:44.022 --> 00:39:47.062
necessarily spoken or even consciously addressed.

00:39:47.062 --> 00:39:51.662
Those of us who report on these subjects are
not told that our stories are undesirable

00:39:51.662 --> 00:39:55.802
because they’re not safe or because they
challenge powers.

00:39:55.802 --> 00:40:00.202
As Sunshine said “News manipulation is subtle.”

00:40:00.202 --> 00:40:05.672
He says “It comes in many forms like withholding
resources such as cameraman and producers

00:40:05.672 --> 00:40:10.472
and conveniently dropping investigative reports
from the newscast when the timing of the show

00:40:10.472 --> 00:40:13.542
happens to run too long to fit it in.”

00:40:13.542 --> 00:40:15.792
We figured it out.

00:40:15.792 --> 00:40:21.222
Ironically management has avoidance response
can result in absurd machinations that inadvertently

00:40:21.222 --> 00:40:24.922
generate the very liabilities they’re trying
to avoid.

00:40:24.922 --> 00:40:28.982
Some of us had boiled it down to a saying
“They’re often worried about the wrong

00:40:28.982 --> 00:40:31.382
things and not worried about the right things.”

00:40:31.382 --> 00:40:35.462
I’ll tell you just one story that serves
as an example.

00:40:35.462 --> 00:40:41.803
During the time under skittish broadcast show
management my last 2 years at CBS I reported

00:40:41.803 --> 00:40:46.202
a story on a credit card scam that showed
a surveillance video of a suspect who was

00:40:46.202 --> 00:40:52.692
caught on camera allegedly using one stolen
card after another at Target, Walmart and

00:40:52.692 --> 00:40:53.692
Macy’s.

00:40:53.692 --> 00:40:58.522
Neither the story nor our use of the surveillance
video was precarious in any sense.

00:40:58.522 --> 00:41:00.961
The police had publicly released the video.

00:41:00.961 --> 00:41:03.032
It already appeared on the local news.

00:41:03.032 --> 00:41:07.422
The suspect had been identified and I had
run my story through the CBS legal department

00:41:07.422 --> 00:41:09.212
for legal clearance.

00:41:09.212 --> 00:41:14.472
Even under the standards of the current skittish
evening news management this story was safe.

00:41:14.472 --> 00:41:18.732
Just prior to air the executive producer views
the finished piece in New York and rings the

00:41:18.732 --> 00:41:22.112
hotline phone to the Washington news room
where I was.

00:41:22.112 --> 00:41:25.432
“We can’t show the suspect’s face.”

00:41:25.432 --> 00:41:26.432
she protest.

00:41:26.432 --> 00:41:27.672
“Why not?”

00:41:27.672 --> 00:41:29.012
I ask.

00:41:29.012 --> 00:41:32.452
She says, “He hasn’t been convicted of
anything.”

00:41:32.452 --> 00:41:34.682
I take a breath.

00:41:34.682 --> 00:41:39.562
The idea that a criminal suspect’s face
can’t be shown on television comes from

00:41:39.562 --> 00:41:42.642
someone who lacks the most basic knowledge
of the law.

00:41:42.642 --> 00:41:47.052
I’m having way too many of these conversations
lately and I know it means she’s not going

00:41:47.052 --> 00:41:48.052
to listen to me.

00:41:48.052 --> 00:41:50.752
She’s scared of the story.

00:41:50.752 --> 00:41:55.382
Nonetheless I patiently explained that somebody
doesn’t have to be convicted of a crime

00:41:55.382 --> 00:41:58.512
for us to identify him or show his face.

00:41:58.512 --> 00:42:03.972
Under her mistaken idea of the law arrest
mug shots would never be shown on the news.

00:42:03.972 --> 00:42:07.882
We wouldn’t have shown OJ Simpson’s face
when he was accused but never convicted of

00:42:07.882 --> 00:42:09.092
murder.

00:42:09.092 --> 00:42:13.452
We wouldn’t have identified any criminal
suspects prior to conviction Timothy McVeigh,

00:42:13.452 --> 00:42:17.512
John Gotti, Jack Kevorkian, Lorena Bobbitt,
Tom DeLay, Martha Stewart, Michael Jackson,

00:42:17.512 --> 00:42:22.312
Bernard Madoff, the Unabomber, Osama bin Laden,
these would be phantom faces that we wouldn’t

00:42:22.312 --> 00:42:23.692
be allowed to show.

00:42:23.692 --> 00:42:27.512
None of these stories wouldn’t have been
done if we were to consistently apply this

00:42:27.512 --> 00:42:30.612
executive producer’s warped view of the
law.

00:42:30.612 --> 00:42:37.072
Still she balks “Call his lawyer and ask
for permission to show his client’s face.”

00:42:37.072 --> 00:42:38.072
Another breath.

00:42:38.072 --> 00:42:43.211
I look at the clock it’s nearly 6:00 story
is set to air at 6:30.

00:42:43.211 --> 00:42:46.632
The odds that I’ll reach the suspect’s
public defender and get a yes from him in

00:42:46.632 --> 00:42:51.792
the next half hour are pretty remote but importantly
the idea that we would set a precedent by

00:42:51.792 --> 00:42:57.632
asking a suspect for permission to use his
image has to rank as one of the more preposterous

00:42:57.632 --> 00:42:59.461
suggestions I’ve ever heard.

00:42:59.461 --> 00:43:01.352
“We don’t need his permission.”

00:43:01.352 --> 00:43:03.862
I reiterate “and I doubt he would give it.

00:43:03.862 --> 00:43:06.262
I wouldn’t if I were him.”

00:43:06.262 --> 00:43:09.532
“Okay,” she says still sounding unconvinced.

00:43:09.532 --> 00:43:14.552
We hang up the phone and within seconds she
sends me a follow-up message telling me to

00:43:14.552 --> 00:43:16.572
blur out the suspect’s face anyway.

00:43:16.572 --> 00:43:20.072
“Just wuz it a little” she says.

00:43:20.072 --> 00:43:23.942
With time ticking I rush back to the editing
booth and break the news to my producer and

00:43:23.942 --> 00:43:24.942
our editor.

00:43:24.942 --> 00:43:26.172
“You’re not going to believe this.”

00:43:26.172 --> 00:43:30.961
I say “but she wants us to get permission
from the defender’s lawyers to use his face

00:43:30.961 --> 00:43:32.711
or else blur it out.”

00:43:32.711 --> 00:43:37.272
“What?” says my producer dumfounded “That’s
outrageous!”

00:43:37.272 --> 00:43:40.792
The producer makes another attempt to reach
the attorney whom we tried earlier.

00:43:40.792 --> 00:43:45.222
It’s futile at this hour and we know that
if asked he’s going to say no anyway so

00:43:45.222 --> 00:43:50.742
we go ahead and blur out the suspect’s face
and re-feed the story to New York to air.

00:43:50.742 --> 00:43:53.472
In some ways this may not sound like a very
big deal.

00:43:53.472 --> 00:43:56.992
What’s the harm in masking a criminal suspect’s
identity?

00:43:56.992 --> 00:43:59.832
I know the answer.

00:43:59.832 --> 00:44:05.182
The executive producer’s misguided and capricious
decision hasn’t avoided risk it’s actually

00:44:05.182 --> 00:44:07.062
created a potential liability.

00:44:07.062 --> 00:44:13.211
We’ve now set a precedent that sets us up
for accusations and bias and inconsistent

00:44:13.211 --> 00:44:18.492
treatment when we don’t do the same for
the next accused criminal suspect.

00:44:18.492 --> 00:44:23.202
This is just one example of the twisted away
sometimes that you get the news or don’t

00:44:23.202 --> 00:44:26.662
get news and I’m happy to take some questions
on that subject.

00:44:26.662 --> 00:44:28.352
Is there anything else that you want to ask?

00:44:28.352 --> 00:44:34.002
Do we have time for questions?

00:44:34.002 --> 00:44:36.832
Speaker 7: Yeah.

00:44:36.832 --> 00:44:39.662
Sharyl Attkisson: Okay.

00:44:39.662 --> 00:44:43.792
Speaker 7: We have some time for questions.

00:44:43.792 --> 00:44:44.792
Microphones will be out.

00:44:44.792 --> 00:44:49.612
Please raise your hand and stand up when they
come to you and once again keep the questions

00:44:49.612 --> 00:44:51.182
shorter than the answer might be.

00:44:51.182 --> 00:44:52.232
We like that.

00:44:52.232 --> 00:44:59.872
Speaker 8: I hate to have to ask this question
but I just wonder if you’ve ever been concerned

00:44:59.872 --> 00:45:06.122
for your personal safety or your life in pursuing
some of the stories that you pursue?

00:45:06.122 --> 00:45:09.852
Sharyl Attkisson: Thank you for asking.

00:45:09.852 --> 00:45:14.552
I don’t want to talk about that but I’m
okay.

00:45:14.552 --> 00:45:15.732
Thank you.

00:45:15.732 --> 00:45:25.402
Speaker 9: In the last week or so the president
did an interview with an online blogger, a

00:45:25.402 --> 00:45:31.902
young woman with bright green lipstick and
it was heralded by Osama as a new era of journalism.

00:45:31.902 --> 00:45:39.032
I just wondered, what do you think this administration’s
target audience are for their release of information?

00:45:39.032 --> 00:45:43.982
Sharyl Attkisson: As I described I think this
administration has perfected the idea of going

00:45:43.982 --> 00:45:51.382
around neutral news reporters who would ask
critical questions and I don’t have a problem

00:45:51.382 --> 00:45:54.232
with doing that thing if that’s what they
want to do.

00:45:54.232 --> 00:46:00.992
My only problem would be if they do that instead
of also being answerable to those who could

00:46:00.992 --> 00:46:06.042
ask the tough and probing and challenging
questions and hold them accountable on behalf

00:46:06.042 --> 00:46:07.182
of the public.

00:46:07.182 --> 00:46:13.092
I think sometimes they do more of that social
media and fun stuff and entertainment celebrity

00:46:13.092 --> 00:46:17.122
stuff which does reach an audience because
that’s how some people get their news in

00:46:17.122 --> 00:46:21.592
a way that they’re not asked critical questions
or they don’t have an informed interview

00:46:21.592 --> 00:46:24.272
on these certain controversial topics.

00:46:24.272 --> 00:46:30.112
Doing that instead of doing more of the other
kind I see is a problem because I’m a traditional

00:46:30.112 --> 00:46:32.892
journalist but I don’t have a problem with
them dogging if they don’t want to do it

00:46:32.892 --> 00:46:35.582
in addition to.

00:46:35.582 --> 00:46:43.142
Speaker 10: Would you present it seems like
a great argument for limited government would

00:46:43.142 --> 00:46:44.142
you agree?

00:46:44.142 --> 00:46:46.752
Sharyl Attkisson: What do you mean by limited
government?

00:46:46.752 --> 00:46:52.972
Speaker 10: You talked about I think 1200
people in media for the Department of Agriculture

00:46:52.972 --> 00:46:57.112
so smaller government and also you get in
the issues of crony capitalism.

00:46:57.112 --> 00:46:59.652
Smaller government reduces those opportunities.

00:46:59.652 --> 00:47:04.082
Sharyl Attkisson: Yeah one might say so.

00:47:04.082 --> 00:47:16.472
Speaker 11: What’s the word on the street
with your brothers and sisters so to speak

00:47:16.472 --> 00:47:18.132
with regard to journalism schools?

00:47:18.132 --> 00:47:20.842
Are they acquiesce or push back?

00:47:20.842 --> 00:47:26.442
Second part, why does Fox kill it in the ratings
every period in your opinion?

00:47:26.442 --> 00:47:31.002
Sharyl Attkisson: I don’t really know about
journalism schools across the board.

00:47:31.002 --> 00:47:38.211
I’ve been thankfully exposed to some great
ones and had dinner sitting next to one here

00:47:38.211 --> 00:47:44.211
from Hillsdale who were trying to teach students
good journalism and fair reporting.

00:47:44.211 --> 00:47:50.152
I think a lot of people go into this business
maybe not coming from journalism school.

00:47:50.152 --> 00:47:54.072
Sometimes they find their way in through a
friend or a contact they study politics and

00:47:54.072 --> 00:48:00.442
government but I’ll say 2 things growing
out from that.

00:48:00.442 --> 00:48:04.652
Some people get in the business and they think
their job is to convince you to feel the way

00:48:04.652 --> 00:48:05.782
they do about something.

00:48:05.782 --> 00:48:06.982
That’s a different kind of journalism.

00:48:06.982 --> 00:48:09.622
That’s not how I was taught.

00:48:09.622 --> 00:48:14.282
If in the end after I presented you with what
the facts are as I’ve been able to find

00:48:14.282 --> 00:48:16.652
them I really don’t care what you decide.

00:48:16.652 --> 00:48:18.832
I’m not here to try to make you think a
certain way.

00:48:18.832 --> 00:48:23.572
I want you to have facts that you can’t
get easily somewhere else.

00:48:23.572 --> 00:48:28.802
Then if you see all those facts and you don’t
care or you think the opposite of other people,

00:48:28.802 --> 00:48:29.942
whatever you think is fine.

00:48:29.942 --> 00:48:31.042
It doesn’t bother me.

00:48:31.042 --> 00:48:35.302
I just like to put those facts on the table
because those belong to you in my opinion

00:48:35.302 --> 00:48:38.802
and you should have those as you make up your
mind.

00:48:38.802 --> 00:48:44.462
Some people want to change your mind but secondly
I would also say believe it or not some of

00:48:44.462 --> 00:48:49.961
the poems I described are not the ground level
reporters and producers who do a great job

00:48:49.961 --> 00:48:56.512
from what I’ve seen day in and day out producing
original stories in an unbiased fashion but

00:48:56.512 --> 00:49:00.872
there are key gatekeepers that shape those
stories and decide which stories will go on

00:49:00.872 --> 00:49:04.442
the air and which ones will never be seen
unless they’re shaped a certain way and

00:49:04.442 --> 00:49:06.422
that’s more of where I see a problem.

00:49:06.422 --> 00:49:09.682
What was your second question?

00:49:09.682 --> 00:49:15.451
Sharyl Attkisson: Fox News has great ratings.

00:49:15.451 --> 00:49:18.922
They can probably tell you more about why
they resonate.

00:49:18.922 --> 00:49:23.382
Just what I hear from people who do … I
know a lot of people that sample.

00:49:23.382 --> 00:49:24.382
They watch Fox.

00:49:24.382 --> 00:49:25.382
They watch MSNBC.

00:49:25.382 --> 00:49:26.382
They click around.

00:49:26.382 --> 00:49:31.132
They want to hear not just yes people do seek
their own opinions but they also want to hear

00:49:31.132 --> 00:49:34.912
what the other people are saying too.

00:49:34.912 --> 00:49:40.492
I think it resonates because maybe it’s
the only network that’s doing that specifically

00:49:40.492 --> 00:49:45.322
where there are several networks doing the
liberal side of it.

00:49:45.322 --> 00:49:50.742
If you want to watch conservative tilted talk
I would say a lot of the reporting is very

00:49:50.742 --> 00:49:55.832
neutral but conservative tilted talk and topics
I can’t think of another TV station that

00:49:55.832 --> 00:49:56.832
you can go to.

00:49:56.832 --> 00:50:01.092
They can get that whole audience in one lump
sum while the rest of the networks divide

00:50:01.092 --> 00:50:06.532
up the liberal tilting audience that’s my
guess.

00:50:06.532 --> 00:50:15.802
Speaker 12: What advice would you give to
pressured journalists and how is the increased

00:50:15.802 --> 00:50:21.772
speed of the internet and the use of social
media impacted investigative and analytical

00:50:21.772 --> 00:50:22.772
journalism?

00:50:22.772 --> 00:50:28.672
Sharyl Attkisson: I don’t have general advice
for journalist you’d have to ask me a question

00:50:28.672 --> 00:50:36.242
about what kind of journalism … what question
what area of advice you’re looking for.

00:50:36.242 --> 00:50:39.792
The impact of social media has been tremendous.

00:50:39.792 --> 00:50:42.162
I think some of it good, some of it bad.

00:50:42.162 --> 00:50:44.592
You can’t unwind and go back from the internet.

00:50:44.592 --> 00:50:48.461
I think some people wish you could.

00:50:48.461 --> 00:50:49.752
Bloggers for example a mixed blessing.

00:50:49.752 --> 00:50:53.711
There have been bloggers that have uncovered
amazing stories that the news media hasn’t

00:50:53.711 --> 00:50:57.922
but by the same token you have to sit there
and sift and filter and be exposed to a lot

00:50:57.922 --> 00:51:01.912
of stuff that may not be true to get to the
truth.

00:51:01.912 --> 00:51:07.312
I don’t have any magic recipe because I’ve
been asked to help you figure out as we try

00:51:07.312 --> 00:51:12.582
to do was there a source that you can believe,
where can you go and you don’t have to knock

00:51:12.582 --> 00:51:17.732
20% off because it’s a conservative site
or 30% off what they say because it’s a

00:51:17.732 --> 00:51:19.082
liberal site.

00:51:19.082 --> 00:51:22.852
It’s very confusing time and I don’t know
what’s going to be born next.

00:51:22.852 --> 00:51:25.583
Something will come all of this and I hope
it’s something good down the road but unfortunately

00:51:25.583 --> 00:51:30.352
I can’t foresee what’s next.

00:51:30.352 --> 00:51:39.532
Speaker 13: You remember MIT economist Jonathan
Gruber who wrote Obama care with full understanding

00:51:39.532 --> 00:51:44.961
that the American people won't be lied to
and he did.

00:51:44.961 --> 00:51:49.942
Could it be that Obama also believed that
we are all idiots that want to be lied to?

00:51:49.942 --> 00:51:54.432
Sharyl Attkisson: It’s hard to say what
conversations are had and what they think

00:51:54.432 --> 00:52:00.892
but the evidence is clear with healthcare.gov
and has not been well reported in my view

00:52:00.892 --> 00:52:04.542
by some it has been but not widely reported.

00:52:04.542 --> 00:52:07.332
I have reported this at CBS News and afterwards.

00:52:07.332 --> 00:52:12.951
The projections showed prior to this passing
the government’s own projections show that

00:52:12.951 --> 00:52:16.262
millions of people were going to be knocked
off their health insurance, that millions

00:52:16.262 --> 00:52:19.402
of people were going to be knocked off of
their work insurance.

00:52:19.402 --> 00:52:21.842
This is happening now and that’s not been
widely reported.

00:52:21.842 --> 00:52:26.662
It’s being overshadowed by positive news.

00:52:26.662 --> 00:52:33.402
I really have no explanation for how the government’s
profession as to what was going to happen

00:52:33.402 --> 00:52:38.451
are so different from what the documentary
record shows they knew was going to happen

00:52:38.451 --> 00:52:40.342
but they’re very much at odds.

00:52:40.342 --> 00:52:47.772
That’s not an opinion, that’s a fact.

00:52:47.772 --> 00:52:52.292
Speaker 14: Thank you so much for your time
this evening.

00:52:52.292 --> 00:52:55.642
My question is going to go back to when you
talked about how it’s important for us to

00:52:55.642 --> 00:52:58.862
be able to make proper decisions to have all
the information really in front of us.

00:52:58.862 --> 00:53:02.842
That you have transparency, that organizations
like the Sunlight Foundation have been very

00:53:02.842 --> 00:53:04.242
adamant in advocating.

00:53:04.242 --> 00:53:09.422
My question is going to lean towards campaign
finance and we see a lot of times where politicians

00:53:09.422 --> 00:53:12.922
oftentimes aren’t able to disclose that
information to the public.

00:53:12.922 --> 00:53:16.862
My question to you would be, what does freedom
of the press have to do with campaign finance

00:53:16.862 --> 00:53:20.762
and how that would relate to an electoral
system that we have in the United States today?

00:53:20.762 --> 00:53:21.762
Thank you.

00:53:21.762 --> 00:53:26.191
Sharyl Attkisson: That maybe a little bigger
question than I’m qualified to answer but

00:53:26.191 --> 00:53:33.282
I would say every time you get at the heart
of campaign finance, what’s wrong with it,

00:53:33.282 --> 00:53:38.122
how to get influence out of it they thought
of another way around that.

00:53:38.122 --> 00:53:40.461
One big area I’ll just give as an example.

00:53:40.461 --> 00:53:45.492
Even if you regulate full transparency and
where certain donations come from and limits

00:53:45.492 --> 00:53:50.042
on campaign contributions and everything you
try to do people can’t go straight from

00:53:50.042 --> 00:53:52.812
congress to lobbying without a cooling off
period.

00:53:52.812 --> 00:53:57.941
There are new tools that come into play that
allow them to get around all of that.

00:53:57.941 --> 00:54:02.772
One tool I think that’s being widely used
I’ve reported a little bit on it are nonprofits

00:54:02.772 --> 00:54:08.642
which are highly in my view unregulated from
a sense of the IRS looking at what they really

00:54:08.642 --> 00:54:12.122
do and what they have to disclose financially.

00:54:12.122 --> 00:54:16.932
Nonprofits are often coopted by special interest
because they sound like altruistic neutral

00:54:16.932 --> 00:54:20.842
organizations but in fact the strings for
them are being pulled and they’re being

00:54:20.842 --> 00:54:27.312
funded by special interest who are in some
way benefiting in some cases a member of congress

00:54:27.312 --> 00:54:28.941
for example.

00:54:28.941 --> 00:54:34.832
I did a story on a couple of years ago a Republican
congressman who started what I would call

00:54:34.832 --> 00:54:44.572
a fake charity that was supposed to collect
money for educational scholarships but hadn’t

00:54:44.572 --> 00:54:45.572
given out any.

00:54:45.572 --> 00:54:49.172
He probably never thought his own connection
with this charity would be revealed and it

00:54:49.172 --> 00:54:52.182
was only revealed through a strange set of
circumstances.

00:54:52.182 --> 00:54:57.852
Once it was revealed he was connected to this
charity that was collecting millions of dollars

00:54:57.852 --> 00:54:59.322
from guess who?

00:54:59.322 --> 00:55:05.812
All the interest regulated by the committee
that he sits on tobacco industry, pharmaceutical

00:55:05.812 --> 00:55:06.812
industry.

00:55:06.812 --> 00:55:11.342
He was golfing and giving access to the people
donating millions of dollars to the charity

00:55:11.342 --> 00:55:16.542
which employed his family members but gave
out no scholarships and this kind of this

00:55:16.542 --> 00:55:18.032
trail went on and on.

00:55:18.032 --> 00:55:23.782
This was a way I believe the money was stated
for that foundation by a pharmaceutical company

00:55:23.782 --> 00:55:29.092
after which this congressman introduced favorable
legislation that made statements on the floor.

00:55:29.092 --> 00:55:33.302
I was able to match up almost every time he
got a large donation from one of his interest

00:55:33.302 --> 00:55:39.461
he was supposed to regulate after a donation
or right before donation he took some action

00:55:39.461 --> 00:55:41.772
or made some statement in congress.

00:55:41.772 --> 00:55:46.532
It took a lot of digging to find that relationship
but to me it was pretty clear and he resigned

00:55:46.532 --> 00:55:48.062
from congress shortly after that.

00:55:48.062 --> 00:55:53.772
That’s just one example I think of it’s
so hard to find an answer to how to get influence

00:55:53.772 --> 00:56:01.482
out and make it be where people who are elected
are serving just the folks instead of the

00:56:01.482 --> 00:56:02.912
well-financed interest.

00:56:02.912 --> 00:56:07.092
Speaker 7: We have time for one more question.

00:56:07.092 --> 00:56:13.652
Speaker 15: Thank you for your insights tonight.

00:56:13.652 --> 00:56:20.052
I’m a concerned citizen outside of the profession
of journalism, politics but interested in

00:56:20.052 --> 00:56:22.662
as much accuracy and objectivity as I can
find.

00:56:22.662 --> 00:56:27.862
I come home from work with about half hour
to an hour of time to invest in that.

00:56:27.862 --> 00:56:32.622
What recommendations would you make to me
about how I should use that time the best?

00:56:32.622 --> 00:56:36.422
Sharyl Attkisson: I’ve been talking about
this a lot with people because I think we

00:56:36.422 --> 00:56:38.312
all have to develop our own ways.

00:56:38.312 --> 00:56:43.682
I can’t say go to one site and that site
will always have the news that’s probably

00:56:43.682 --> 00:56:44.682
the way you want it.

00:56:44.682 --> 00:56:49.422
I don’t think that exist and yet many sites
and many news outlets do great reporting.

00:56:49.422 --> 00:56:55.342
Maybe it’s harder fought these days but
PBS frontline, 60 minutes, the networks, the

00:56:55.342 --> 00:56:58.522
newspapers they’re still doing great investigative
reporting.

00:56:58.522 --> 00:57:03.062
You have to find reporters you trust maybe
on a particular topic because of some experience

00:57:03.062 --> 00:57:07.182
you’ve had that knows that its ringing true
and then you may decide that’s reporter

00:57:07.182 --> 00:57:13.052
you will follow on other topics because you
found them to be in your view trustworthy.

00:57:13.052 --> 00:57:17.582
Same as with some news outlets I think you’ll
hop around from one place to the next and

00:57:17.582 --> 00:57:20.472
it’s not going to be the same … You just
spend your 30 minutes looking at the same

00:57:20.472 --> 00:57:21.792
3 or 4 places every time.

00:57:21.792 --> 00:57:23.451
I just don’t think it works that way.

00:57:23.451 --> 00:57:28.812
A lot of people are telling me that they use
their Twitter feed and Facebook feed as story

00:57:28.812 --> 00:57:32.212
circulate on outlets they might never have
seen.

00:57:32.212 --> 00:57:36.532
They pop through those and they find stories
of interest and that’s their daily newspaper

00:57:36.532 --> 00:57:37.532
of the day.

00:57:37.532 --> 00:57:41.402
They’re just looking around and seeing what
people are sitting around and talking about.

00:57:41.402 --> 00:57:46.461
I will repost in the next couple of days something
that I posted a couple of week ago because

00:57:46.461 --> 00:57:51.852
people are asking what some alternative websites
you might want to check out that do some good

00:57:51.852 --> 00:57:53.052
investigative journalism.

00:57:53.052 --> 00:57:56.252
You may not like everything they do but they
do some great work.

00:57:56.252 --> 00:57:59.762
Just as one example I’ll say Project Censored.

00:57:59.762 --> 00:58:04.482
It’s probably projectcensored.com but you
can google it.

00:58:04.482 --> 00:58:09.622
They made it their mission to take stories
that are untouchable that news outlets for

00:58:09.622 --> 00:58:14.902
whatever reason, for a variety of reasons
don’t want to touch and they report on those

00:58:14.902 --> 00:58:20.102
and at the end of the year they compile the
10 best stories into a book and publish it.

00:58:20.102 --> 00:58:23.592
These are stories you probably won't see anywhere
else for a lot of different reasons and there

00:58:23.592 --> 00:58:25.632
are places like that that you might find interesting.

00:58:25.632 --> 00:58:30.132
I’ll repost that in the next week or so
in sharylattkisson.com and you can see if

00:58:30.132 --> 00:58:34.242
there’s anything of interest there for you.

00:58:34.242 --> 00:58:34.762
Thank you all.

